    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Since these nutrients are being released from the soil particles, what are some of the practical signs or symptoms we'd see in the vine foliage if we're seeing that manganese toxicity you mentioned? Like, what does the deficiency vs the excess actually look like on the leaves?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Manganese deficiency in grapevine foliage typically presents with interveinal chlorosis, where the area between the leaf veins turns yellow while the veins remain green. As the deficiency progresses, you may also see necrotic spots or dark spots on the leaf surface, alongside leaf curling or deformed growth. In contrast, manganese toxicity can cause similar symptoms to iron toxicity, often manifesting as speckling of the leaf tissue, which appears as small, dark spots around or on the veins. While these symptoms may easily be confused, iron toxicity predominantly shows on younger leaves, whereas manganese toxicity typically appears on mature or aging foliage. Both deficiencies and toxicities are mirrored by chlorosis, but the affected areas and specific symptoms differ. It's important to evaluate symptoms in conjunction with soil and leaf tissue tests to accurately identify the issue and adjust management practices accordingly.",
      "source": "target",
